slides.body_md:
Two carbon atoms can share **one, two or three pairs** of electrons:

- **single bond** C–C (one pair), as in ethane, C₂H₆;
- **double bond** C=C (two pairs), as in ethene, C₂H₄;
- **triple bond** C≡C (three pairs), as in ethyne, C₂H₂.

In every case each carbon still makes **four bonds in total**: a double bond counts as two and a triple bond as three.

In **position isomerism**, the carbon chain is the **same**, but the **double or triple bond** or the **functional group** sits at a **different position**:

- but-1-ene CH₂=CH–CH₂–CH₃ and but-2-ene CH₃–CH=CH–CH₃ (C₄H₈);
- but-1-yne and but-2-yne (C₄H₆);
- propan-1-ol and propan-2-ol (C₃H₈O).

Propene and propyne have **no** position isomers: moving the bond to the other end gives the same molecule.
